WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.840 --> 00:00:08.110 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  as humans we fortified war for centuries  film literature and epic poems even in  sculpture if you live in a major city  chances are there's a memorial nearby  commemorating soldiers long dead and  then there's our digital medium and in  videogames as well war is all around us  we've stormed the beaches at Normandy  defended Earth from alien threats we've  launched nuclear weapons fought the  Crusades and battled mythical foes like  many of the best stories the best games  thrive on conflict and as far as  conflict goes if you can match the  bombast and spectacle of all-out war  it lets us command armies shoot digital  guns and more often than not blow [ __ ]  up all in the name of a greater cause  but at what point does this become  redundant at what point do games use  worn-out for storytelling but merely as  a backdrop for heroics and violence at  what point does this glorification of  war get old if you're like me you've  heard the story dozens of times before a  squad takes on an army or a lone hero  save the day  and if you're like me you can appreciate  when a game deviates from this formula  when a games not afraid to take a deeper  look at war but causes realities and  consequences of it a few standout titles  have done just that  and with call duty returning to world  war two later this year and Battlefront  two story being told from the Empire's  perspective it's worth looking back to  figure out how we can move forward  [Music]  war in video games have coexisted for a  while it goes back to the arcade days  with cabinets like space invaders or  Missile Command games that despite their  technical limitations frame their simple  gameplay mechanics with a surrounding  conflict then came 1981 and with it  Castle Wolfenstein we were dropped into  a historical war a pixelated  representation of an actual time period  it would be the first of dozens of  titles set during World War two for a  while this was the norm I can't tell you  how many times I stormed Omaha Beach  defended the Russian front or dropped  into Holland to end the war before  Christmas I have relatives that fought  in World War two but these games never  bothered me on a personal level the era  was modern enough to interest me but far  enough away to ignore its realities I  like the rhythm of these games I like  the way the weapons fell in my digital  hands but that all changed in 2007  US Marines stationed on high alert  unlike previous Call of Duty games  modern warfare eschewed the world war 2  setting for that of a fictional conflict  spanning Russia Ukraine in the Middle  East it tells a story about terrorism  and nuclear weapons combining the fears  of the 21st century with those of the  Cold War but here's the thing about  modern warfare in 2007 for the first  time I was playing a shooter about a  current conflict yes the plot was  fictional but many of its missions drew  clear inspiration from events unfolding  in Iraq and Afghanistan  my brother was in Iraq at the time with  the US Marines I can't remember the  exact moment but at some point it dawned  on me  modern warfare had taken real-world  horror and transformed it into fun  imagine if they had video games in 1944  picture a 16 year old sitting in front  of his TV controller in hand invading  France while his older brother huddled  in a foxhole in Belgium this isn't too  far from what happened in 2009 developer  atomic games announced six days in  Felucia historical games set in Iraq in  2004 because of how recent the events  were the studio and publisher Konami  drew ire from the public and raise  questions about the ethics of such a  project it was subsequently canceled  under the pressure and hasn't seen the  light of day since now don't get me  wrong I love Call of Duty 4  in fact I'm playing the remaster right  now and even in 2017 it's fantastic but  my experience all those years ago raised  questions like should games like this  even be fun  is it ok to make these experiences into  trophies leaderboards and achievements  with the war games like this say about  our society or culture in general  after playing this game for the first  time in years many of my questions still  remain as it turns out I wasn't the only  one with these questions Matthew Thomas  Paine a professor at the University of  Notre Dame wrote a book about military  video games titled playing war in it he  explores how military games such as Call  of Duty battlefield and Tom Clancy's  Rainbow six have transformed  contemporary conflicts into games how  they portray military tactics yes but  also how they perpetuate widespread  political beliefs look back at the  evolution of military shooters in a vast  majority of them were fighting Germany  Japan or fascist Italy as paine  clarifies in his book these games give  us the virtual chance to stand up to our  historical enemies to be the heroes  delivering the final blow to the age-old  villains but in our post 9/11 world  things changed more frequently military  shooters asked us to wage war against  radical terrorists from places across  the world we watched CNN and saw footage  from Fallujah and Ramadi clips of  Fighters with RPGs and ak-47s we turn on  our consoles and saw the same thing this  highlights how games can reflect a  culture of our time for better or worse  our collective hopes anxieties and fears  whether they be through a realistic lens  or one tinted with fiction these are  stories that millions of people consume  and spend billions of dollars on each  and every year and this isn't consigned  to military shooters sci-fi titles like  Halo 2 and Gears of War expressed the  same fears of foreign invasion of the  enemy coming to our doorstep in halo 2  the aliens have found earth in Gears of  War the locusts have emerged from  beneath our feet these games may not  have the burden of realism on the backs  of their stories but in the end they are  telling very familiar stories and of  course many of these games glorify the  events at hand it's hard to make a war  game that doesn't inherently romanticize  war we play most games for fun escapism  or a sense of accomplishment the vast  majority of shooters are by nature going  to sensationalize their conflicts  this is nothing new film is done this  for decades in literature for centuries  once more unto the breach dear friends  valiant stories of brothers and arms  fighting the forces of evil with God on  their side authors such as Ernest  Hemingway Joseph Heller and Kurt  Vonnegut published anti-war novels that  broke down the structure and themes of  the works that came before them the film  world saw a similar change in the tide  the french director francois truffaut  once said it was impossible to make  anti-war movies with all of their  excitement and sense of adventure they  inevitably make war look fun but then  Oliver Stone made platoon Francis Ford  Coppola made Apocalypse Now  they made movies about the horrors and  realities of war and the personal toll  it can take on the human psyche and then  we have video games  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  in a lot of ways we are still stuck in  the past games at least most of them are  made to be fun they're made with a clear  set of rules that dictates how we  function within their worlds and in turn  within the wars they depict and many  shooters do a great job of creating fun  I enjoy the hell out of them but when I  look at other titles I'm confident we've  actually taken those first few steps  into more meaningful war stories as  Payne says in his book games more than  any other medium can pose that difficult  question when if ever is it ok to pull  the trigger few games embody that choice  more than spec ops the line at the end  of the game you realize your character  is created imaginary villain to justify  the horrific things you did in the chaos  of war and by giving you the chance to  kill the villain or spare him the game  is asking you whether you think those  actions were righteous more than that  SpecOps highlights the consequences of  war while most shooters actively ignore  them in one sequence developer Jaeger  actually makes you walk through an area  you previously shelled with white  phosphorus only to discover that you  killed dozens of civilians in the  process spec ops doesn't ignore the  ripple effects of your violence neither  does this war of mine in fact this 2014  game focuses entirely on the civilian  side of things as your group fights to  outlast the siege of a fictional city  you upgrade your shelter scavenge for  supplies and heal other survivors while  the war outside rages on the only way to  win is to survive until a ceasefire is  called and as civilians that decision is  completely out of your hands you're at  the mercy of a conflict you can't  control and then there's The Witcher 3  Wild Hunt and despite the RPGs fantasy  setting this is very much a game about  war not the battles and heroics but the  politics confusion and results of war we  speak to soldiers on both sides  each one of them believing they're  absolutely in the right we see a  landscape covered in corpses  we fight creatures that feed on the  guilt and despair of war The Witcher 3  may not show us raging battles but it  does show us the bitter aftermath in a  massive open world  to be clear I'm not saying every game  needs to say something profound about  war or the way games relate to it I'm  still gonna play this year's Call of  Duty and I'm still enjoying titanfall 2  and battlefield 1 but 2016 did see the  release of numerous shooters many of  them depicting war in one way or another  this is a pervasive genre and it shows  no signs of slowing down  war and video games are cousins I just  don't think they have the healthiest  relationship right now  that's why games such as spec ops and  The Witcher 3 are so important like any  form of art or entertainment video games  can reflect the anxieties and  aspirations of our time to act like  these multibillion-dollar properties  don't play a part in our collective  culture would be a disservice to the  medium many of us love to act like games  don't affect our perspective on war  would be to ignore their broader  potential and with battlefront 2 and the  new Call of Duty releasing later this  year I think we need to keep asking  those difficult questions and I think we  need to keep moving forward hey everyone  thanks so much for watching this week's  episode of reboot next week Jake and I  will be doing a Q&amp;A about this episode  which focus more on war and will also be  teasing our next episode episode 6 so in  the meantime get your youtube comments  and tweet at Jake Decker or me Mike  McCarty and we might feature you on our  show see you then  [Music]

